type: software
title: "The innermost reliability zeros of complete graphs and Waldvogel's constant"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. The all-terminal reliability polynomial R_n(q) of the complete graph K_n is the probability that K_n stays connected when each edge fails independently with probability q. The smallest modulus h_n of a complex zero satisfies -n log h_n = 2 log n - 2(1 + log W_0) + o(1), where W_0 = 0.7377507574... is the rotation-restricted Whittaker constant studied by Waldvogel in 1983. Interval arithmetic shows that the innermost zeros approach the directions arg q = +-2.3932... (about +-137.1 degrees), not the negative real axis. On the arc pi/30 <= arg q <= 59pi/30, carrying 29/30 of the zeros asymptotically, a joint limit law for angle and rescaled radius holds with an explicit profile. For all sufficiently large n the negative real axis carries exactly one zero when n = 0, 3 (mod 4) and none otherwise (checked exactly for 2 <= n <= 24). A short criterion answers a question of Buys: zero-counting measures of the spanning reliability of loopless matroids converge to the uniform measure on the unit circle exactly when rank/size tends to zero."
